    "body": "Blue Yonder ’s 2026 Consumer Retail Returns Survey found that 46% of U.S. shoppers who purchased an AI-recommended product returned one within the past year. Products that differed from their descriptions were the leading reason.\nWhat’s Related\nThe survey found that 24% of U.S. consumers have bought something following an AI recommendation. Among those who returned an AI-recommended purchase, 36% cited a mismatch with the description, while 23% reported an incorrect size or fit.\nThose results differ from returns overall, where size or fit led at 42%, followed by products differing from their descriptions at 20%. The findings measure shoppers who returned an item, rather than the percentage of AI-recommended purchases returned.\nProduct isn't what shopper expected\nBlue Yonder CEO Duncan Angove attributed the findings to problems with the product information available to AI shopping tools.\n“When what arrives doesn’t match what the shopper expected, it goes back in the box.”\nYounger shoppers were more likely to buy through AI recommendations. Adoption reached 43% among Gen Z and 36% among millennials, compared with 19% of Gen X and 9% of baby boomers. Among Gen Z shoppers who bought an AI-recommended product, 56% had returned one.\nRELATED STORIES: Nine Ways to Keep Retail Returns From Taking Over the Warehouse | Buy More, Keep Less: Gen Z’s Shopping Habit Tests Retail Returns\nStricter policies can cost retailers and customers\nReturn policies also influenced where respondents shopped. More than half (51%) chose one retailer over another because it offered a better policy, and 29% stopped shopping with a retailer that tightened its rules in the past year.\nMeanwhile, 82% said they would shop less often or stop entirely if a retailer restricted their returns because they returned purchases too frequently.\nShoppers accept alternatives\nNearly two-thirds of U.S. respondents (63%) would not pay a return fee. Free return shipping and fast refunds were their leading priorities.\nHowever, 51% would accept store credit instead of returning an item. Another 55% had been told to keep an unwanted product during the past year, and 67% of that group said the experience made them more likely to shop with the retailer again.\nA third-party provider conducted the survey in September 2026 among more than 5,000 consumers in five countries, including 2,000 Americans.",
